A||N||13||Ptrelinski||Considering Fiero...||||1||
Z||000000||Ptrelinski||04-29-2001||09:55 PM||||Hi guys,<P>Im 18 and recently felt the urge to stop using my parents cars and get one of my own. I really like the Fiero and the possibilty of turning it into a kitcar sometime in the future is cool. My concern is that Fiero's are old, and i have very little practical experience with cars. I do know a fair bit from car magazines, but like I said nothing practical. I am willing to learn and I have taken an interest in mechanics, reading a lot on the net. Anyway, since they are so old, will I be able to deal with all the problems? and will it cost me a fortune for parts or even service? what about insurance rates compared to others?<P>The fiero in question would probably be an '86 GT, and I found a few for 3000 - 5000$ cdn.<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-29-2001||10:11 PM||||Welcome!<P>Well, I am 17 and I have learned all I have about cars from reading about them and doing all the work myself.  I have just completed a very big resto, took apart most of the car and put it all back together better than it was.  I have learned a lot just from reading many of the topics here on the forum.<P>As long as the engine runs strong in the car you're looking at, you probably wont have to replace or fix anything that would be too difficult.  You can probably do most things yourself, as in general tune-up things like plugs, wires, air/fuel filter changes, changing oil and filter, and things like that.<P>If you have trouble pinpointing a problem with your car, someone here will probably be able to tell you what to fix.  You'll never find any better source for information about your car in the world than right here on this forum.  Many common problems have been discussed many times too, you can use the search function to find old topics that contain keywords about your problem.<P>I hope you get that car, so you can see how much fun they are!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||04-29-2001||10:24 PM||||i just bought an 85 SE last friday, so I dont have any experiece with them yet. But from what I have seen and heard, the biggest risk and potential headache, is the electronic fuel injection system. There are several sensors and valves and injectors... and when one or more fails the car runs like crap and can be further damaged by running it that way.<P>  If you got the cash then its not a real big deal to replace this kinda stuff. The grief of it is: another car, with a carburator, coils, points and condensor setup, if you take care of the car, and maintain the engine, then things likes carbs dont just fail for no apparent reason. A more basic car is more predictable and what it does is a direct result of what you do to it.<P>  But it looks like the electronic fuel injection and electronic ignition stuff On a Fiero DOES fail for no apparnet reason.<P>  The reason I bought a Fiero is cause I have two really dependable cars now, a 94 Saturn SL2 (HomeComing Special) and a 2000 SL, and there are 3 of us here that need a car. So I figure I can afford to have a vehicle that is questionable when it comes to reliabilty when I have 2 other dependable cars. i can use the Fiero for short trips around town, and dont have to worry about being without wheels if it is offline for a while.<P>   The other reason I bought a Fiero is because it is such a great looking and fun to drive machine. I am hoping that will make up for the reliability headaches.<P>  But who knows. I think fieros are like Neons in one respect. Some Neon owners love their cars and have no trouble with them at all, others think their Neon is the car from hell! It seems to be the same way with Fieros. Some owners swear by them and others swear at them.<P>  I guess it depends on what you are looking for. A sporty little car, a dependable means of transportation, a chick magnet...<P>  Something else to keep in mind. Everyone pretty much agrees that its impossible to 'get lucky' in a Fiero. The center consule is also affectionately refered to as the 'birth control device'!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||04-29-2001||10:27 PM||||Welcome! You are wise to consider the age of the cars.  Wait for a well cared-for specimen. It will pay off in the long run. Buy the best car you can for the money.  If there are problems make sure you feel you can handle them. Mechanically, they are not too advanced.  With a good manual and some guidance you CAN do most anything.  Just take your time and go step by step.  Lots of the guys here haven't seen a rusty CDN car so make sure the nooks and crannies look okay.  Parts are usually not a problem as long as you don't need it yesterday (of course you will). <BR>When you find a possible car, see what it needs and check to see how much $$ and trouble it will be. (like $$$$clutch$$$$$)<BR>My son is 17, can't find his a$$ with both hands but has taken his Fiero apart and put it back together (hasn't fixed it yet, mind you).  They're bags o' fun but beware, they're addictive.<P>Gary||||reg||12
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-29-2001||10:32 PM||||Fiero's can be a little bit daunting sometimes.  They have a bad reputation for being hard to work on, having electrical problems, fires, etc, etc.<P>While some of the reputation is mildly deserved, the rest of it is mostly just bad rumors (like the fire part).  A Fiero isn't any more likely to catch fire than any other car that isn't cared for properly.<P>If you can find someone in your area that is a Fiero-nut, or at least good with cars, I'd say you should definitely buy the car.  I've driven quite a few cars and this one is probably one of the most fun behind the wheel.<P>If you're going to be learning the hands-on part of auto care on your own... well it's your call.  If you think you can learn by reading here, then go for it.  If not, you might want to consider getting a car that's a bit easier to care for.  Taking a Fiero to a mechanic is a crap shoot.  They may or may not know how to fix the car right, and they'll probably overcharge you to boot.<P>Once again though, you'll find the people here more than willing to help you out.  The world needs more Fiero-fanatics, and the battle-hardened veterans on this board are quite happy to help a new Fiero owner learn.  (I know, as I just recently got my first Fiero and my constant and undoubtedly annoying questions were not ignored.)<P>Just one final word of warning:  Fiero's can be habit forming.  Many people own more than one, and some own a dozen or more.  Then again, I can't say as I've heard of anyone wanting to give them up, either.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||04-29-2001||10:44 PM||||Thats a good point. If you are thinking of buying a fiero, dont get one unless YOU are gonna be the mechanic. In fact i would say that for any older car.<P>  If you have to take the car to a garage and pay $50 or more per hour every time something is not right with it, you are better off getting a used Del Sol or Miata, or something that is renown for its reliability and quality.<P>  Old cars are just as much a hobbie and a past time as they are a means of transportation. unless you have the bucks, plan on doing all the work you can yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GoCartGuy||04-29-2001||11:06 PM||||Welcome to the forum.  Just wanted to add a few tidbits from my limited knowledge.  First off, as far as home mechanic work yea it can be daunting, but remember we're here to help and if you're lucky you'll find a nice guy on the forum with plenty of knowledge in your area.  Don't forget to check the other sites in the web ring for a Fiero owners club near you.  Second Fiero's are addictive, I haven't even finished paying off the loan for my first one '85 GT and I'm already looking for a second.  They're fun, safe and reasonably reliable cars, just take care of it and it'll take care of you.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||CanadianGT||04-29-2001||11:07 PM||||Hi,<BR>Welcome!<BR>I am an 18 year old guy from Toronto as well.  I have had my Fiero for a little over a year now.  I worried about the same problems you did, and I can tell you, that as long as your willing to learn, and dont mind getting dirty every once in awhile, A fiero can be a great car to own.  Insurance rates are not that bad for us around here, and parts are not that expensive  and are fairly easy to come by.  In addition, they are not very sophisticated thus making them fairly easy to work on.  If you have any problems, people here are always willing to help as well!<BR>Good luck, and happy motoring!<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000008||treyb79||04-30-2001||05:03 AM||||Hey,<P>To echo another post, wait for a nice one to come along.  I know how it can be, believe me ... I'm a type-A personality ... I'm not patient... but after a very long wait I came across my '88 Formula V6/5sp just last monday.  Was it worth the wait?  You bet!!<P>As for learning the in's and out's of auto mechanics, I'll share with you my experience.  I'm 22.  At 18, I undertook my first major task on a vehicle.  (In other words, I'd often done simple things like brake pads, oil, filters, etc, just nothing along these lines).  I decided to take my '92 S-10 Blazer, yank out the stock 4.3 V6, and swap it with a 355ci V8 small block chevy.<P>Was it tough?  Hell yeah.  Were there times that I wished I could just push it to the side of the road and hope someone would take it away from me?  Of course.  But after 4 months of blood, sweat, and tears - it was done.  And I learned more then about working on cars than any book could have ever taught me.  Now I can't seem to get the black off of my hands, b/c I'm always into something.  :-)<P>Good luck finding your ride.  And of course, if you get one and run into anything sticky, this board is filled with very knowledgable people who will be more than willing to lend assistance.<P>Trey<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ptrelinski||04-30-2001||05:29 PM||||Well thanks a lot guys, im quite impressed with all of the responses I got. they will definately be remembered when im weighing all the pros and cons of the fiero.<P>Anyway, Ive got a few months to decide (ill be buying at the end of summer) so ill monitor this forum and keep an eye out for good fiero's. Ill also read up on it, myabe buy one of those chiltons manuals. One final question, should i be worried about high mileage cars? I notice Fieros with high mileage (like 160k miles+) are significantly cheaper then lower mileage cars.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroFanaticus2||04-30-2001||06:04 PM||||Almost any car that has high miles will be cheaper.  If you look around a little you could most likely find a GT with 40-50K on it for less than $5000.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||10:53 PM||||Every car has its own little quirks. Its a lot more fun to have a $3000 Fiero than a $6000 Honda. Yes, any older cars going to need things, Just check out what you buy carefully before laying out the cash. Most parts on Fiero are way cheaper and easier to come by too, lots of generic parts. Make sure it runs smoothly, clutch works properly, and has no exhaust leaks and youve eliminated 3/4 of the problems. Most of the other problems are annoying, but relativly cheap to fix.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||05-01-2001||01:22 AM||||I find that older cars can be MORE reliable than some of the newer ones.<BR>A huge problem with the majority of Fieros is that the previous owners didn't:<P>a) do proper maintainance, <BR>b) had absolutely no mechanical ability,<BR>c) didn't have a good mechanic at a shop to assist in maintaining the car,<BR>d) just plain didn't care for the car-(put in gas, add oil when low and drive the sh_ _ out of it!),<BR>e) when it broke, drive it anyway to create more problems.<P>As with any used car be sure to check it out well, due to your location, get with some of the long time owners to help you with information on what, and where to look for it at.<BR>I have been a Fiero owner for 13+ years and have found that once you get it right, they will run well and never let you down with proper PM. Problems will arise just like any other car.<BR>An issue of fire was brought up in a previous response, this involved mainly the '84s and early V-6 cars. Recall work should have been done long ago. If it's still here now, I don't think that it will burn up!<P>Within the last 6 weeks, I have driven an '87 GT from central CA home with absolutely NO problems! If you don't mind the import fees, check out Fiero Dave's web site (link at bottom of page) For some good deals!<BR>I will be listing about 3 on there by the end of the month, including the one from CA.<P>Good luck with your decision!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||KRMFiero||05-01-2001||06:47 AM||||here is a big thing to cheak for, pull the trunk carpet down on the drivers side ( pull it down until the second trunck thingy and see if there is any rust, if it is rotted threw there is a frame on the other side that probley is rotted to and well thats not a good thing, just look for that i think everyone else covered everything else you may want to know||||reg||1
